# id: BM105-clause-one-ignores-the-snapshot-below
# blinds: clause 1 asking about the tombstone AT EACH OBSERVABLE SEQUENCE
# covering-lane: cpp-test
# expect: killed
# control-lane: cpp-build
# expect-control: alive
#
# THIS PATCH IS BUG-004 EXACTLY, PRESERVED -- AND WHAT IT PRESERVES IS THE
# SIMPLIFICATION, NOT THE INVARIANT.
#
# THE SPECIFIC EDIT THAT PRODUCES IT, so a refactorer recognises their own hand:
#
#   "NewestCoveringTombstone(tombstones, user_key, s) does not obviously depend
#    on the loop, and calling it once per observable sequence is O(|S|) work
#    inside a hot loop. Hoist it out. Use the interval's top as the sequence,
#    since that is the most a reader could see."
#
# Every clause of that is reasonable and the conclusion is wrong. The call DOES
# depend on `s` -- `s` is the sequence AT WHICH the tombstone must be visible --
# and a tombstone at 9 is invisible to a snapshot at 5 even though both lie
# inside a version-at-4's interval.
#
# STATING THE EDIT AND NOT ONLY THE RULE IS THE POINT. A refactorer believes
# they are preserving the invariant -- "a version hidden by a tombstone is not
# required" -- and they are. What they are not preserving is WHICH SEQUENCE THE
# QUESTION IS ASKED AT, which the invariant's wording does not mention.
#
# It conflates two different sequences. A snapshot at 5 and a tombstone at 9 are
# both "inside the interval" of a version at 4 -- and the tombstone is INVISIBLE
# to the snapshot. The version is the answer at 5, and this drops it: DATA LOSS
# for a snapshot below a range delete.
#
# It is invisible to every end-to-end test, because a live snapshot holds the
# pre-compaction tables RESIDENT and reads through them. The loss appears at the
# next Open, after the snapshot is gone. Only a unit-level assertion on what the
# compaction EMITTED can see it, which is why one exists.
